rsetup-board-agent — notes for Debian users
===========================================

What this package provides
--------------------------

  /usr/bin/rsetup-board-agent                board-side agent binary
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/rsetup-board-agent.service
  /var/lib/rsetup-board-agent                durable task journal and board identity
                                             key (state directory, mode 0700)

Not shipped on purpose: /etc/default/rsetup-board-agent (controller address and trust
pins) — see the configuration section below.

The unit is installed but neither enabled, started, nor restarted on upgrade
(dh_installsystemd --no-enable --no-start --no-stop-on-upgrade): no maintainer
script of this package runs `deb-systemd-invoke start`/`restart`/`stop` on the
install or upgrade paths, so installing or upgrading never brings the agent up
and never stops an agent you are already running. Removing the package may stop
a running agent — that is the prerm remove branch, which this package does not
control (from debhelper 13.25 the generated snippet always stops the service on
remove; official debhelper changelog, Debian bug #1079181). Installing and
upgrading do not reload the systemd manager either, so reload it yourself before
the first enable:

  sudo systemctl daemon-reload
  sudo systemctl enable --now rsetup-board-agent
  journalctl -u rsetup-board-agent -f

Read this before you enable it
------------------------------

This package delivers the agent's read-only half over the real tunnel, and nothing
more:

  * The unit starts the production mode --connect: an active outbound TCP connection
    to the controller, a v3 client handshake that verifies the controller against the
    pins you configure, an encrypted record layer, then the dual-stream session
    (read-only actions plus journal queries). Business requests are accepted on the
    control stream only, only for the six allow-listed actions, and the controller
    identity is the one authenticated by the handshake — a key self-declared in a
    packet or in the payload is never trusted. A disconnected session is retried with
    exponential backoff 1/2/4…60 seconds plus sub-second jitter, reset only after a
    session actually reaches Ready.
  * A signed revocation or an approval denial stops the reconnect loop and exits 0, so
    Restart=on-failure does not bring the agent back; the agent also records an
    UNAUTHORIZED marker (mode 0600) next to its identity key. A kick is not a
    revocation: it reconnects after backoff.
  * A real host reboot boundary is NOT implemented. The unit injects no reboot
    dispatcher, so device.reboot.prepare and device.reboot.execute answer "capability
    unavailable" (1004) and the advertised capability list does not claim reboot is
    executable; the binary and the unit never reboot the machine.

Enabling the unit therefore gives you a board that answers read-only queries from a
pinned controller, not a controllable board. Do not record the control path (in
particular reboot) as closed-loop on the strength of this package.

Required configuration: /etc/default/rsetup-board-agent
-------------------------------------------------------

The package deliberately does not ship this file — the controller address and the
trust pins are site material. The unit declares it as a *required* EnvironmentFile
(no leading "-"), which fails closed in two different ways that must not be confused:
until you create the file, systemd fails the unit before ExecStart (the log line is
"Failed to load environment files: No such file or directory") — the agent binary
never runs, so it produces no exit status of its own. Exit status 78 (configuration
error) is what the binary itself returns once the file exists but a required variable
is missing or invalid. Both are fail-closed behaviour, not a warning to ignore, and the
unit deliberately does not make the file optional. systemd reads it literally, line by
line: no shell, no $expansion, no command substitution; anything that is not KEY=VALUE
must be a # comment.

  # /etc/default/rsetup-board-agent — template, replace every PLACEHOLDER
  RSETUP_CONTROLLER_ADDR=controller.example.internal:8443
  # Required. Comma-separated 64-char lowercase hex public keys you trust. There is
  # no trust-on-first-use: with no pin configured the agent refuses to start rather
  # than trusting whatever answers.
  RSETUP_CONTROLLER_PUBLIC_KEYS=<64-hex-pin>[,<64-hex-pin>]
  RSETUP_BOARD_KEY_FILE=/var/lib/rsetup-board-agent/board.key
  RSETUP_BOARD_JOURNAL_DIR=/var/lib/rsetup-board-agent
  RSETUP_BOARD_DEVICE_SN=<serial>
  RSETUP_BOARD_DEVICE_MODEL=<model>
  RSETUP_BOARD_FW_VERSION=<firmware-version>
  RSETUP_BOARD_EXTRA_INFO=<free-form>

Notes on the two paths: RSETUP_BOARD_JOURNAL_DIR must already exist (the agent never
creates its state root; StateDirectory creates /var/lib/rsetup-board-agent as 0700)
and holds the durable task journal written with real fsync/rename persistence.
RSETUP_BOARD_KEY_FILE is loaded once at startup as mode 0600 inside a 0700 directory;
a symlink or a foreign owner is rejected. Per-connection handshakes still generate
fresh randomness every time — the loaded key is identity, not a session key. After
editing the file: sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart
rsetup-board-agent.

Running user: root
------------------

The unit runs as User=root/Group=root, without a dedicated unprivileged account
and without DynamicUser. The agent reads the whole board's hardware and system
state (/proc and /sys temperatures, load, memory, the kernel boot_id, and the
firmware/partition/power sources that will be added later); those sources are not
uniformly readable by an unprivileged user, and running as one root process under
the sandbox below is preferable to widening a sudo/polkit allow-list per reading.

Because the process is root, the unit keeps the surrounding restrictions:
ProtectSystem=strict makes /usr and /etc read-only for it, ProtectHome=yes hides
home directories, PrivateTmp=yes, NoNewPrivileges=yes and LockPersonality=yes are
set, and /var/lib/rsetup-board-agent (created 0700 by StateDirectory) is the only
path it may write to. RestrictAddressFamilies permits IPv4/IPv6/Unix sockets only —
--connect needs outbound IPv4/IPv6 to the controller and binds no listening socket at
all; every other address family (packet, bluetooth, …) stays forbidden.

Stopping is graceful: SIGTERM drains in-flight requests for up to 5 seconds, joins the
session task and exits 0 within TimeoutStopSec=30s. Joining is only claimed when it is
confirmed: if the session task still cannot be confirmed reclaimed after the abort
escalation, the agent emits a driver-unreclaimed event instead of pretending the task
was joined. On the SIGTERM path it still exits 0 (a graceful stop must not turn into a
failure that Restart=on-failure would relaunch); an unconfirmed reclaim outside a
requested shutdown is reported as a local fault with exit status 78 and the loop stops
instead of opening another session with a task that may still be running.

Local, non-systemd runs
-----------------------

Run by hand, the binary runs as whoever invokes it (no privilege elevation of any
kind). Besides --connect, three local modes remain available and are **not** the
production path:

  rsetup-board-agent --once
  rsetup-board-agent --watch --interval-secs 10
  rsetup-board-agent --loopback-test-server --listen 127.0.0.1:39876

The last mode is a test-only JSON-lines console on loopback; it is not the
production wire protocol, it authenticates nothing, and it must not be exposed. The
three modes and --connect are mutually exclusive — a local mode never opens a tunnel,
and --connect never serves the JSON console. Some readings require root to be
meaningful, which is why the packaged unit runs as root while a manual run stays
with the invoking user.
